    rdfs:comment "An Appointments Clause claim is a legal argument asserting that a particular appointment or nomination made by a government official violates the Appointments Clause of the United States Constitution. This claim contends that the appointment was not made in accordance with the constitutional requirements outlined in the Appointments Clause, which typically involves the need for Senate confirmation or compliance with other specified procedures, rendering the appointment or nomination invalid or unconstitutional." ;
